% ============================================================================
% capitalization-rule — common English capitalization rules and what each
% one actually requires (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Capitalization is governed by a small set of named rules, each covering
% a different trigger for the capital letter — where a word sits in a
% sentence, what pronoun it is, or what kind of noun it names. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? capitalization_rule(pronoun_i, $D)
%       % capitalized_anywhere_in_sentence
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `capitalization_rule(rule, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any rule not one of these three source-cited
% examples. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall
% which rule it names:
%
%     ? capitalization_rule($R, capitalize_first_letter)
%       % first_word_of_sentence
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Capitalization Rules and
% Examples" article, which states each rule directly:
%
%     rule (this table's atom label)   description (this table's atom label)      rule quote (verbatim)
%     -------------------------------  ------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     first_word_of_sentence           capitalize_first_letter                     "Whenever you start a sentence, capitalize the first letter of the first word."
%     pronoun_i                        capitalized_anywhere_in_sentence             "Capitalize I when using it as a pronoun anywhere in a sentence."
%     proper_noun                      capitalized_regardless_of_position           "Proper nouns are always capitalized in English, no matter where they fall in
%                                                                                     a sentence."
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing (twice, across two cycles of this loop).
% `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable, widely-cited grammar-content
% company, the same tier this stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov
% language sources and the same source family already cited by
% `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/`contraction.adj`/
% `possessive-noun.adj`/`simile-meaning.adj`/`prefix-meaning.adj`. A rule
% not one of these three — `quotation`, a real capitalization rule the
% same article covers in its "Capitalization and quotes" section, but not
% one of this table's three rows — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table capitalization_rule {
    columns rule, description

    row (first_word_of_sentence, capitalize_first_letter)
    row (pronoun_i, capitalized_anywhere_in_sentence)
    row (proper_noun, capitalized_regardless_of_position)

    source "Whenever you start a sentence, capitalize the first letter of the first word."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/punctuation-capitalization/capitalization-rules/"
    trust consensus
}
